# gkvm guests

Scaffolded by `gk init`. A **guest** is a small deterministic program (rv64im ELF) that a
contract runs through the gkvm precompile with `GkVm.exec`; its identity on-chain is
`programHash = keccak256(guest.elf)`.

## What `gk init` added

| path | what | yours to edit? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `guest/{{EXAMPLE_GUEST}}` | {{EXAMPLE_DESC}} | yes |
| `guest/crt/`, `guest/link.ld` | gk-guest-crt, vendored: entry code, hostcalls (`gkvm.h`), linker script. Part of every guest's `programHash` | no — changing it changes every programHash |
| `{{SRC}}/gen/{{BINDING_NAME}}.sol` | generated binding (`PROGRAM_HASH` + `call`) | no — `gk build` rewrites it |
| `{{SRC}}/{{CONSUMER}}` | sample consumer | yes |
| `{{TEST}}/{{CONSUMER_TEST}}` | test wired to `GkVmFfiShim` | yes |
| `foundry.toml` | `[profile.gkvm-ffi]` appended (`ffi = true` lives there and nowhere else) | — |
| `remappings.txt` | `{{SDK_REMAP}}={{SDK_PATH}}/src/` | — |
| `.gitignore` | `cache/gkvm/` (build outputs, shim spill files) | — |

Re-running `gk init` is safe: existing files are never overwritten.

## Quickstart

1. **Build the guest** (docker: the prebuilt `ghcr.io/gas-killer/gk-toolchain` image, or
   `ubuntu:24.04` + apt-get when it cannot be pulled; C guests also build with a host
   `riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc`):

       python3 {{SDK_PATH}}/tools/gk build guest/{{EXAMPLE_GUEST}}

   → `cache/gkvm/build/<name>/guest.elf` + `{{SRC}}/gen/{{BINDING_NAME}}.sol`. The build is
   reproducible: same source + crt + toolchain → same `programHash`.

2. **Get `gk-run`**, the sidecar that executes guests (the same binary the operator path wraps).
   One line (prebuilt, sha256-verified; also installs a `gk` command on `~/.gk/bin`):

       curl -fsSL https://gaskiller.xyz/bash | sh

   Or from a checkout of `gas-killer/gas-analyzer`:

       cargo install --locked --path crates/gkvm --bin gk-run    # → ~/.cargo/bin/gk-run

   Keep `--locked`: the executor is a consensus pin and `Cargo.lock` is what holds its
   dependency tree still (cargo may warn that some locked crates are yanked and suggest dropping
   the flag — don't). This builds the x86_64 JIT tier. The JIT is x86_64-only; add
   `--features portable-exec` for the portable interpreter, the tier operators treat as the
   reference. Outputs and cycle counts are identical on both; the `"tier"` field of the JSON line
   `gk-run` prints on stderr says which one you have. Both install as `gk-run` — pass
   `--root <dir>` (→ `<dir>/bin/gk-run`) to keep them side by side.

   Once the gkvm crate is on gas-analyzer's default branch, no checkout is needed:

       cargo install --locked --git https://github.com/gas-killer/gas-analyzer gas-analyzer-gkvm --bin gk-run

3. **Run the tests** with the guest really executing:

       forge test --match-contract {{TEST_CONTRACT}}

   `gk init` installed a `forge` prehook into `~/.gk/bin`: inside this project it exports
   `GK_RUN`, selects the `gkvm-ffi` profile, rebuilds any guest you edited (content hashes,
   not mtimes) and announces itself in one `[gk]` line on stderr before the real forge runs.
   `gk test --match-contract {{TEST_CONTRACT}}` is the same thing without the PATH shim
   (`gk test` = rebuild-if-stale + `GK_RUN=<sidecar> FOUNDRY_PROFILE=gkvm-ffi forge test …`),
   and `GK_FORGE_PLAIN=1` gives you the untouched forge. Without a sidecar the shim-backed
   tests skip (plain `forge test` stays green and ffi-free).

4. **Run it on a local node** — `gk anvil` starts anvil with the gkvm precompile and every guest
   built here installed; deploy the consumer with `GKVM_ADDRESS` (`0x3559…3c59`) as its `gkvm` and
   `cast call` it. The precompile exists on that node and on no real chain (see below).

## Writing your own guest

**Python:** add `guest/<name>.py` with a typed `main` (`int`, `bool`, `bytes`, `str`,
`list[...]`, `tuple[...]` returns), then `gk build guest/<name>.py` — the type hints become
the binding's Solidity signature. Runs under the gkvm MicroPython port: unbounded integers, no
floats, no imports beyond the frozen stdlib subset. `gk init --python` scaffolds one.

**C:** add `guest/<name>.c` (`#include "gkvm.h"`; `gk_input_read` → compute → `gk_output_write`;
`gk_abort` for a typed, deterministic failure), then `gk build guest/<name>.c` and import
`{{SRC}}/gen/Gk<Name>.sol`. Guests are freestanding rv64im C: no libc, no floating point (nothing
links libgcc), no I/O beyond the hostcalls in `guest/crt/gkvm.h`.

`forge clean` deletes `cache/` and with it the built ELFs — rebuild with `gk build`.

## What works today, and what does not

**Today a fresh project gets the forge emulation, and nothing else.** Read this before planning
around a guest.

What you have: in forge tests the guest really executes — `GkVmFfiShim`, a test-only contract,
decodes the precompile's wire format and shells out to `gk-run`. Outputs, typed guest errors and
cycle counts are the real ones (the sdk's golden fixtures hold the shim bit-identical to direct
`gk-run`). One thing is approximate: unless the test pins it with `setCycleLimit`, the shim
derives the cycle budget from `gasleft()` after its own overhead, so an out-of-cycles boundary
does not sit exactly where an operator would put it.

What you do not have: execution anywhere outside forge tests. The gkvm precompile never exists
on a chain — not on mainnet, a testnet, a fork, or a vanilla anvil. Its address is an empty
account there, so `GkVm.exec` reverts `GkVmUnavailable` (the always-on test in
`{{TEST}}/HelloGk.t.sol` asserts exactly that). This is by design: guests run only inside Gas
Killer operators' pinned simulation environment, and the chain only ever sees the signed state
diff. For a consumer to be served by live operators, all of the following still has to happen —
none of it has shipped:

1. **The operator-side precompile lands.** It exists on unmerged gas-analyzer branches stacked
   on draft PRs; a released gas-analyzer does not know the gkvm address.
   Tracking: [gas-killer/gas-analyzer#197](https://github.com/gas-killer/gas-analyzer/issues/197).
2. **The service rolls it out.** Operators must run the `local` executor with your guest ELF
   (and any artifacts) installed, addressed by `programHash`. Not started.
   Tracking: [gas-killer/service#451](https://github.com/gas-killer/service/issues/451).
3. **Your consumer is registered with `requiresGuestVm`.** Without that gate, an operator that
   lacks the program does not fail — it deterministically simulates the `GkVmUnavailable`
   revert and could sign *that* as the transition. With it, such an operator abstains. The
   gate is part of the same service work (#451).
4. **Disputes.** Guest results are not yet bound into the fraud-proof path, so a guest-backed
   transition is not slashable today. Tracking:
   [BreadchainCoop/sp1-contract-call#22](https://github.com/BreadchainCoop/sp1-contract-call/issues/22).

The design itself is still a draft
([gas-killer/solidity-sdk#85](https://github.com/gas-killer/solidity-sdk/pull/85); sdk tracking:
[gas-killer/solidity-sdk#84](https://github.com/gas-killer/solidity-sdk/issues/84)). The wire
format, error selectors and address this scaffold uses are the pinned ones, but until the spec
is ratified treat a `programHash` as provisional: it commits to the vendored crt, and a crt
change changes every hash.
